In this lesson we'll look at general solutions and the period of trig equations. When you solve something like sine of theta equals a number, you usually find one specific angle first. But sine and cosine keep repeating every full turn of two pi, so that one angle is really just the start of an endless family. To get the rest, you just add or subtract the period over and over. For sine and cosine, the period is two pi, so the general solution is your starting angle, plus or minus two pi times any whole number. Tangent works the same way, except its period is only pi, so you add or subtract pi instead. Without a domain, the equation has infinitely many solutions, which is why textbooks almost always box the problem into a range like zero to two pi. If you ever see a number sitting inside the bracket, like sine of two theta, just divide your starting angle by that number, but remember the period shrinks to two pi divided by that same number too. Keep those two ideas, family plus domain, and these equations become much friendlier. Now let's see it in action.